From: Protector of Earth Recomp
Subject: [PATCH] GS CPU backend: per-primitive pixel and sampler setup, per-pixel steps inlined, Z test before the frame read

What it is for:
  In Ben 10: Protector of Earth's first level the GS is the limit. Under
  callgrind, on the first 60,000 commands of a replay of the level's GS
  commands, WritePixel was 20% of the instructions: for every pixel it
  worked out the buffer bases, formats and VRAM accessors again, and it,
  SampleTexture, combineTexture, wrapTextureCoordinate and
  classifyAlphaTest were calls whose entry and exit cost a visible share.
  SampleTexture also decoded CLAMP, the texture size and the palette key
  for every sample, and converted each of the four bilinear texels with
  its own format switch.

The changes:
  - PixelSetup (buffer bases, FBW, formats, the frame and Z read and
    write accessors) and SamplerSetup (texture size, wrap modes and
    region, whether the format is indexed, the palette cache key) are
    made once per primitive by DrawTriangle and DrawSprite;
    WritePixelWith and SampleTextureWith take them. WritePixel and
    SampleTexture keep their signatures and make the setup per call.
  - BeginSampling checks the palette cache key once per primitive. The
    cache only memoises LookupCLUT, whose result depends on the CLUT
    buffer (a CLUT load resets the key) and the key's own fields, so
    emptying it at the start of a primitive instead of at its first
    sample gives the same texels.
  - The four bilinear texels are converted with one format switch.
  - WritePixelWith, SampleTextureWith, combineTexture,
    wrapTextureCoordinate and classifyAlphaTest are forced inline
    (PS2_GS_FORCEINLINE).
  - WritePixelWith tests Z before reading the frame: both reads only look
    at memory, and a failed Z test or destination alpha test ends the
    pixel either way.
  Same results bit for bit.

How it was checked:
  The project's GS replay (tools/gs_replay, findings/20 s.25): the level
  recording gives the same GS memory hashes at all 35 points and at the
  end; four random recordings with draws sampling their own buffers give
  the same hashes at 20 points each as before this patch; on four random
  recordings 2 and 6 row bands give the same GS memory as GSCpuBackend.
  callgrind, first 60,000 commands of the level recording: 15.84 G ->
  12.36 G instructions (12.36 G includes 0.40 G of the replay's own
  loading). ps2xTest 454/454.

